---
// apps/site/src/components/home/TheName.astro
//
// The name, as one column of type: eyebrow, headline, the story in two
// stacked paragraphs, the line to remember, and the etymology boxed under it.
//
// There is no illustration. Four were tried in a left column here — a quipu,
// a drawn runner, a relay diagram, a wordmark plate — and every one asked the
// reader to look at something before reading the sentence that already
// carries the whole idea. Two of them also broke in the browser, because they
// painted an SVG from scoped CSS and both halves have to arrive for anything
// to show.
---

<section class="name" id="name">
  <p class="eyebrow">the name</p>
  <h2>A runner, ahead of the road.</h2>

  <p>
    The <em>chasqui</em> was a relay runner of the Inca road network, carrying
    messages between waystations across the Andes.
  </p>
  <p>
    laqi plays the same role for your team. It carries the agreed API contract
    before the real backend implementation exists.
  </p>

  <p class="pull"><em>laqi carries the contract before the real API arrives.</em></p>

  <div class="roots">
    <p class="roots-label">roots</p>
    <div>
      <p class="roots-text">
        Quechua — <em>llulla</em>, false · <em>chasqui</em>, messenger
      </p>
      <p class="roots-aside">say it out loud: it sounds like “lucky”</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .name {
    max-width: 76rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 4.5rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 2.25rem;
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 3.8vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  /* The paragraphs run the section's width rather than a narrower measure:
     the design sets this section wide and lets the two short paragraphs
     carry it, which is what keeps it from reading as a column of prose
     dropped into a product page. */
  .name > p {
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
    color: var(--dim);
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--viotx);
  }

  .pull {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0 !important;
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.2vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--viotx) !important;
  }

  /* A panel rather than a rule. The etymology is an aside, and boxing it
     says so without needing a heading — and keeps it visually separate from
     the metaphor above, which is not derived from it. */
  .roots {
    display: flex;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--bd);
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 35%, transparent);
  }

  .roots-label {
    flex: none;
    width: 5rem;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .roots-text {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .roots-aside {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--faint);
  }
</style>
